<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taifa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Taifa refers to the independent Muslim kingdoms into which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> fragmented after the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> in the early eleventh century. Weak and often at war with one another, the taifa kings paid tribute to Christian rulers. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire</a> describe how their vulnerability, made clear by the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> in 1085, led to Almoravid intervention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> and the eventual absorption of the taifas into the Almoravid state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taifa.jpg" alt="Taifa" width="320" height="250" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Falconaumanni · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taifas2.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
